  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1158 AM CDT Fri Oct 16 2026\n\nTonight \n\nHigh pressure, currently located over the Northern and Central\nPlains, will build quickly east across the area tonight. As \nthis occurs, the surface pressure gradient will slacken. The\ncombination of light winds and clear skies will result is strong\nradiative cooling. As typical with the NBM, it looked too warm \nin central Wisconsin so blended in some MOS. Low temperatures \nwill range the mid-20s to lower 30s. There may be even a few \nlocations in the lower 20s in central Wisconsin.\n\nWeekend\n\nThe surface high pressure area will shift east into the Great\nLakes and into the Mid-Atlantic this weekend. As this occurs,\nthe winds will become southerly and increase into the 10 to 20\nmph. High temperatures will warm into the mid- and upper 50s on\nSaturday and into the lower and mid-60s on Sunday.\n\nMonday through Friday \n\nA 500 mb ridge will build across the region early in the week\nand it will keep the area dry and unseasonably warm through the\nremainder of the week. High temperatures will be mainly in the \n60s through Wednesday and around 70s Thursday and Friday. Low \ntemperatures in the 30s and 40s through Wednesday and 40s and \n50s Thursday and Friday.",
